hey I've talked about Saudi Arabia many  many times Saudi Arabia is one of the  closest allies of the United States it  is one of the most extreme religious  regimes authoritarian religious regimes  in the world it is similar to Hamas in  many ways where women must have in Saudi  Arabia the consent of a male guardian to  travel abroad they often need permission  to work or study a woman must have  permission from her father brother or  other male relative in the case of a  widow sometimes it's from her own son to  obtain a passport to leave the country  many workplaces and universities demand  that a woman have a Guardians consent  just to be employed at the University or  to have a to be a student at the  University although that's not legally  required if you rent an apartment or you  want to get treatment at the hospital  you often are also required to have your  male Guardians permission well there are  at least some women in Saudi Arabia who  want to change this 14000 Saudi women at  least 14,000 Saudi women have signed a  petition calling for an end to the male  guardianship system this is good news I  unfortunately don't know whether  anything will really change number one  will the government listen to a petition  from 14,000 women I don't know  I'm skeptical about that but number two  as we've talked about with the burqa  there are very very conspicuous loud  boisterous advocates of the male  guardianship system in Saudi Arabia that  are women and we've said yes sometimes  you hear well the women want the burqa  well they want it because they actually  have really considered in a serious  manner what the burqa represents and  what it means and the implications of it  or they want it because from the time  they were born they were raised in a  society where the burqa is described it  is associated with morals and ethics and  the right social order  so I have significant doubts that this  is really going to achieve anything I  think it is fantastic that even 